# One pass

- Start with the `apps/api` composition root, the `packages/axc/rest` and `packages/axc/application-services` entries and nearest existing feature, and the `packages/axc-verification` acceptance entry. Use one batched search to identify them, then read them in parallel.
- Use at most one follow-up read batch to resolve a specific unknown. A failed check permits the reads needed to fix it.
- Do not reopen a file or successful command, summarize intermediate work, or start a second exploration, review pass, or subagent.
- Write each new file once. Make a follow-up edit only to resolve a specific unknown or failed check.
- Ponytail and Caveman apply in full. This rule limits tool calls only.

## Scoped edits

- Change only files required for the current unknown or failed check.
- Do not broaden an edit into adjacent features, cleanup passes, or unrelated refactors.
- Prefer one follow-up edit on an already-touched file over opening a new file.
- Do not edit unrelated tests to satisfy a type or shared-context shape change when a shared test helper can absorb it.

## Validation

- Format only the changed paths once with `pnpm exec biome check --write --no-errors-on-unmatched <paths>`.
- While iterating, run only the package test file(s) or describe filter that cover the files just edited, through `node scripts/agent-check.mjs -- <narrow command>`. Prefer `pnpm exec vitest run <relative-test-file>` in the affected package, or `pnpm exec turbo run test --filter=<affected-package> --` with vitest args that select that file. Include `pnpm run knip` in the affected-package preflight when exports or package boundaries changed.
- Do not run the full monorepo unit suite, full acceptance, or `pnpm run verify` until that narrow check passes.
- Then run `pnpm run verify` once through the same wrapper. The wrapper rejects another full verify after a pass and allows one rerun only after a failed verify.
- Do not run acceptance or the full suite as a separate command. They run inside `pnpm run verify`.
- On failure, fix the reported cause, rerun only the failing narrow test or failed package through the wrapper, then run one more wrapped verify.

## Command output

- Trust the wrapper's `PASS` result. Do not inspect its successful log.
- On failure, use its first error block. Read more of the temporary log only when that block cannot identify the cause.
- Final reply: changes, commands, pass or fail, limitations, dependency justification, and security findings. Do not narrate the work.
